+### Dotted option names with strict mode
+
+When using `.strict()` with dotted option names, disable `dot-notation` and
+use literal dotted keys in the configuration:
+
+```js
+import yargs from 'yargs';
+const argv = yargs([])
+  .parserConfiguration({'dot-notation': false})
+  .option('auth.username', {type: 'string'})
+  .config({'auth.username': 'test'})
+  .strict()
+  .parse();
+
+console.log(argv['auth.username']); // 'test'
+```
+
+The same key form can be used in a JSON config file:
+
+```json
+{
+  "auth.username": "test"
+}
+```
+
+With the default `dot-notation` setting, both nested configuration objects
+and dotted keys produce a nested `auth` object, which strict mode can report
+as `Unknown argument: auth`. Disabling `dot-notation` keeps the parsed keys
+flat, so access the value using `argv['auth.username']` rather than
+`argv.auth.username`.
+
